Option A — Use Domain-Wide Delegation (DWD)
Backend acts as the user, so no sharing is needed.
How it works
Plain text
User pastes Sheet URL
   ↓
Backend extracts sheetId
   ↓
Service account impersonates user@company.com
   ↓
Reads sheet via API (no sharing)
What you need
Google Workspace (company domain)
Admin enables DWD
In code:
Java
credentials.createDelegated("user@company.com");
Pros
✔ No manual sharing
✔ Clean UX (URL paste works directly)
✔ Works with user-owned sheets
Cons
❗ Needs admin approval
❗ Higher security scrutiny
🥈 Option B — OAuth (User Login)
User logs in with Google → you use their token.
Flow
Plain text
User login with Google
   ↓
Paste URL
   ↓
Backend uses OAuth token
   ↓
Reads sheet
Pros
✔ No sharing
✔ No admin setup
Cons
❗ Token handling (expiry, refresh)
❗ More implementation effort
🥉 Option C — Change ownership model (best long-term)
Don’t let users own the sheet.
Flow
Plain text
Backend creates sheet (service account owns)
   ↓
Shares with user
   ↓
User edits
   ↓
Backend reads (no sharing needed later)
Pros
✔ Fully controlled
✔ No dependency on user sharing
✔ Simplest backend
Cons
❗ Doesn’t support “user’s own sheet” scenario
